  "reasoning": "The CAP theorem is a fundamental concept in distributed systems that states that it is impossible for a distributed data storage system to simultaneously guarantee all three of the following properties: consistency, availability, and partition tolerance. This theorem was first proposed by Eric Brewer in 2000.",
  "final_answer": "• Consistency: A system must ensure that all nodes in the system see the same data value for a given data item. This means that if one node writes data, all other nodes must see the same data value before they can write it back. • Availability: A system must ensure that every request receives a response, without guaranteeing the response contains the most recent version of the data. • Partition tolerance: A system must be able to continue functioning even when there are network partitions, where some nodes in the system cannot communicate with each other. It is impossible for a system to simultaneously guarantee all three of these properties.",
